      "nets": [
        "GND"
      ],
      "observed": "The isolated CAN transceivers U10 and U11 have both GND1 and GND2 pins connected to the same board GND net, and their secondary supplies/connector grounds are also referenced to that same ground.",
      "expected": "For the intended isolated CAN buses, GND1 (primary side) and GND2 (isolated CAN side) must be on separate ground domains; tying them together defeats galvanic isolation.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(node (ref \"U10\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"GND1\") (pintype \"power_in\"))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(node (ref \"U10\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GND2\") (pintype \"power_in\"))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(node (ref \"U11\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"GND1\") (pintype \"power_in\"))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(node (ref \"U11\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GND2\") (pintype \"power_in\"))"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "two isolated CAN buses (`CAN0`, `CAN1`), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller (`U8`, `U9`) through an isolated CAN transceiver (`U11`/companion)"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The CAN interfaces would not be galvanically isolated; ground loops, common-mode noise, and fault currents could propagate between the rover ground and external CAN devices, contrary to the intended isolated CAN architecture.",
      "confidence": 0.88
    },
    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": [
        "D4",
        "D14",
        "D23"
      ],
      "pins": [
        "D4.2",
        "D14.2",
        "D23.2"
      ],
      "nets": [
        "/LED_3.3V_POWER_SW"
      ],
      "observed": "The net /LED_3.3V_POWER_SW connects only the anodes of D4, D14, and D23; it has no supply, power symbol, or driver node.",
      "expected": "A 3.3 V LED power net should be connected to +3V3 or a switched 3.3 V supply so the LEDs can be powered.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"32\") (name \"/LED_3.3V_POWER_SW\") (class \"Default\") (node (ref \"D14\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"A\") (pintype \"passive\")) (node (ref \"D23\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"A\") (pintype \"passive\")) (node (ref \"D4\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"A\") (pintype \"passive\")))"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The PI activity and Ethernet status LEDs driven from this net cannot light, so status indication would differ from the intended design.",
      "confidence": 0.74
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The files show at least two strong connectivity anomalies: USB-C VBUS is shorted to D-, and the isolated CAN transceiver grounds are tied together, defeating the intended CAN isolation. A third likely issue is an open 3.3 V LED power net."
}

